    "id": "06-work-energy-and-power",
    "prefix": "06",
    "number": 6,
    "title": "Work Energy and Power",
    "classTag": "11 Physics",
    "weightage": "High",
    "rawMarkdown": "# Work Energy and Power\n\n- Class: 11 Physics\n- Chapter no.: 6\n- Weightage: High\n\n> The work–energy theorem replaces force–time analysis with force–displacement analysis. Use it whenever speed at a position is asked.\n\n## Kinetic Energy & Work–Energy Theorem\n\n1. Kinetic energy $K=\\tfrac12mv^2$ (scalar, ≥ 0). Work by a constant force: $W=\\vec F\\cdot\\vec d=Fd\\cos\\theta$. Positive if $\\theta<90^\\circ$, zero if ⊥, negative if $\\theta>90^\\circ$.\n\n$$K=\\tfrac12mv^2=\\frac{p^2}{2m},\\qquad W=Fd\\cos\\theta$$\n\n2. Work–energy theorem: the work done by all forces (conservative, non-conservative, pseudo) equals the change in KE.\n\n$$W_{total}=\\Delta K=\\tfrac12mv^2-\\tfrac12mu^2$$\n\n## Calculation of Work; Variable Forces\n\n3. Variable force: work $=\\int\\vec F\\cdot d\\vec r$ = area under the $F$–$x$ graph.\n\n$$W=\\int_{x_1}^{x_2}F(x)\\,dx$$\n\n4. Spring force $F=-kx$. Work done by the spring from $x_1$ to $x_2$:\n\n$$W_{spring}=\\tfrac12k\\left(x_1^2-x_2^2\\right)$$\n\n5. Work done by gravity is $mg\\times$(fall in height), whatever the path. Work done by a normal force or tension can be non-zero when the surface or support moves.\n\n> Given $v=f(x)$, the work done between two points is just $\\Delta K$. You don't need the force (2026 shift). Penetration/resistive-force questions: $mg(h+d)=F_{av}d$ for a ball falling into sand.\n\n*Asked: 2026 • 6 Apr • Evening; 2026 • 2 Apr • Evening; 2022 • 25 Jul • Evening; 2024 • 27 Jan • Evening*\n\n## Potential Energy, Conservative Forces\n\n6. A force is conservative if its work is independent of path (equivalently, zero work around any closed path): gravity, spring, electrostatic. Friction and air drag are non-conservative.\n7. Potential energy is defined only for conservative forces:\n\n$$\\Delta U=U_f-U_i=-W_{conservative},\\qquad F=-\\frac{dU}{dx},\\quad \\vec F=-\\nabla U$$\n\n8. Equilibrium where $dU/dx=0$. Stable: $U$ is minimum ($d^2U/dx^2>0$), restoring. Unstable: $U$ is maximum. Neutral: $U$ is constant.\n9. Conservation of mechanical energy: if only conservative forces do work, $K+U$ = constant. With non-conservative forces:\n\n$$W_{nc}=\\Delta(K+U)$$\n\n## Gravitational & Spring PE, Forms of Energy, Power\n\n10. Near the earth $U=mgh$. A spring stretched or compressed by $x$ stores $U=\\tfrac12kx^2$.\n\n$$U_g=mgh,\\qquad U_{spring}=\\tfrac12kx^2$$\n\n($F=-kx=-dU/dx$.)\n\n11. Power: rate of doing work. Unit: watt (1 hp = 746 W). Efficiency = output power / input power.\n\n$$P=\\frac{dW}{dt}=\\vec F\\cdot\\vec v,\\qquad P_{av}=\\frac Wt$$\n\n12. Constant power engine starting from rest: $Pt=\\tfrac12mv^2$ ⇒ $v\\propto t^{1/2}$, $x\\propto t^{3/2}$. For uniform acceleration from rest, instantaneous power = 2 × average power.\n13. Energy has many forms (mechanical, thermal, electrical, chemical, nuclear, mass-energy $E=mc^2$). The total energy of an isolated system is always conserved.\n\n> Power with a time-dependent force: find $v(t)$ from $\\int F\\,dt/m$, then $P=\\vec F\\cdot\\vec v$. Also common: $p=\\sqrt{2mK}$ ratio questions (\"KE increased by 300% → momentum doubles\").\n\n*Asked: 2025 • 7 Apr • Morning; 2026 • 4 Apr • Morning; 2024 • 8 Apr • Morning; 2021 • 20 Jul • Evening*\n\n## Collisions\n\n14. In any collision, momentum is conserved (the impulsive forces are internal). KE is conserved only in elastic collisions.\n15. Coefficient of restitution (along the line of impact): $e=1$ elastic, $0<e<1$ inelastic, $e=0$ perfectly inelastic (bodies stick).\n\n$$e=\\frac{\\text{velocity of separation}}{\\text{velocity of approach}}=\\frac{v_2-v_1}{u_1-u_2}$$\n\n16. 1-D elastic collision:\n\n$$v_1=\\frac{m_1-m_2}{m_1+m_2}u_1+\\frac{2m_2}{m_1+m_2}u_2,\\qquad v_2=\\frac{2m_1}{m_1+m_2}u_1+\\frac{m_2-m_1}{m_1+m_2}u_2$$\n\n- Equal masses exchange velocities.\n- Heavy body hits a light one at rest: the light one moves off with ≈ $2u$.\n- Light body hits a heavy one at rest: it rebounds with ≈ the same speed.\n- Fraction of KE transferred to $m_2$ (at rest): $\\dfrac{4m_1m_2}{(m_1+m_2)^2}$.\n\n17. Perfectly inelastic: common velocity and KE loss:\n\n$$v=\\frac{m_1u_1+m_2u_2}{m_1+m_2},\\qquad \\Delta K=\\frac{m_1m_2}{2(m_1+m_2)}(u_1-u_2)^2$$\n\n18. Ballistic pendulum: a bullet embeds in the block (momentum conserved), then the block rises (energy conserved):\n\n$$h=\\frac1{2g}\\left(\\frac{mv}{m+M}\\right)^2$$\n\n19. 2-D elastic collision: conserve momentum along both axes. Equal masses with one at rest move off at 90° to each other.\n20. Ball dropped from height $h$ with restitution $e$: rebound height $e^2h$, after $n$ bounces $e^{2n}h$ (heights $h,\\ e^2h,\\ e^4h,\\ \\ldots$).\n\n> A spring between two blocks is compressed most when they share a common velocity, not when one is at rest.\n\n*Asked: 2025 • 3 Apr • Evening; 2021 • 17 Mar • Evening*\n\n## Impulse & Impulsive Force\n\n21. Impulse = change in momentum = area under the $F$–$t$ graph. Impulsive forces are large forces acting for a very short time (bat on ball). During such short times, finite forces like gravity can be ignored.\n\n$$\\vec J=\\int\\vec F\\,dt=\\Delta\\vec p$$\n\n22. Ball hitting a wall normally and rebounding with the same speed: $\\Delta p=2mv$. Average force $=2mv/\\Delta t$. Machine gun: $F=nmv$ (n bullets per second).\n\n> Impulse is asked often: $F$–$t$ graph area, wall rebound ($2mv$), machine-gun recoil ($F=nmv$).\n\n*Asked: 2023 • 1 Feb • Evening; 2023 • 31 Jan • Morning; 2023 • 11 Apr • Morning*\n\n## JEE Main Focus\n\n**Question patterns asked again and again**\n- Resistive force / penetration / stopping distance — e.g. 2022 • 25 Jul • Evening; 2024 • 27 Jan • Evening\n- $p=\\sqrt{2mK}$ relations (% change in KE vs momentum) — e.g. 2024 • 8 Apr • Morning; 2021 • 20 Jul • Evening\n- Constant power: $x\\propto t^{3/2}$ — e.g. 2024 • 5 Apr • Evening; 2021 • 18 Mar • Morning\n- $U(x)$ graph, $F=-dU/dx$, equilibrium type — e.g. 2026 • 21 Jan • Morning; 2024 • 29 Jan • Morning\n- Spring–block energy (compression, speed) — e.g. 2025 • 8 Apr • Evening; 2024 • 8 Apr • Evening\n- Assertion–reason on conservative forces — e.g. 2025 • 7 Apr • Evening; 2025 • 28 Jan • Morning\n\n**Concepts combined in one question**\n- Spring + incline + friction\n- Projectile + KE\n- Collision + energy loss (collisions)\n- Energy + Lorentz force (magnetic force does no work, see Moving Charges and Magnetism)\n\n**Tricky points**\n- Work by friction on an incline is $-\\mu mg\\cos\\theta\\cdot d$.\n- Kinetic friction does negative work on the system as a whole (it produces heat), but static friction can do positive work on a body.\n\n**Mistakes to avoid**\n- Using $mgh$ for heights comparable to the earth's radius (use $-GMm/r$).\n\n**Asked in April 2026**\n- 2 Apr S2 • Q36 — Average resistive force of sand: mg(h+d) = F•d\n- 4 Apr S1 • Q46 — W = F_net • s with vector displacement\n- 4 Apr S1 • Q49 — Power P = F•v with time-dependent force (v from impulse)\n- 5 Apr S2 • Q28 — Work done by static friction on incline moving at constant velocity\n- 6 Apr S2 • Q29 — v = f(x): work = ΔKE\n"
