    "id": "08-gravitation",
    "prefix": "08",
    "number": 8,
    "title": "Gravitation",
    "classTag": "11 Physics",
    "weightage": "High",
    "rawMarkdown": "# Gravitation\n\n- Class: 11 Physics\n- Chapter no.: 8\n- Weightage: High\n\n> Newton's law of gravitation is universal. With field, potential and energy it explains $g$, satellites, escape and Kepler's laws.\n\n## Law of Gravitation & G\n\n1. Every particle attracts every other particle with a force along the line joining them:\n\n$$F=\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2},\\qquad G=6.67\\times10^{-11}\\ \\text{N m}^2\\text{kg}^{-2}$$\n\n2. Gravitational forces obey superposition. A uniform spherical shell or sphere attracts an external particle as if all its mass were at its centre. $G$ was measured by Cavendish (torsion balance).\n\n(Force vs $r/R_E$: hollow shell gives 0 inside; solid sphere gives $\\propto r$ inside.)\n\n## Gravitational PE & Potential\n\n3. PE of two particles (zero at infinity), and potential = PE per unit mass:\n\n$$U=-\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r},\\qquad V=-\\frac{GM}{r}$$\n\n| Distribution | Potential $V$ |\n|---|---|\n| Point mass / outside sphere or shell | $-GM/r$ |\n| Ring on axis (distance $x$) | $-\\dfrac{GM}{\\sqrt{a^2+x^2}}$ |\n| Inside spherical shell | $-GM/a$ (constant) |\n| Inside uniform solid sphere | $-\\dfrac{GM}{2a^3}(3a^2-r^2)$; centre $=-\\dfrac{3GM}{2a}$ |\n\n(For a solid sphere, $V$ at the centre is $-1.5\\,GM/R$.)\n\n4. For several masses add the PE of every pair. Work done by an external agent = change in PE.\n\n## Gravitational Field\n\n5. Field = force per unit mass. Relation with potential:\n\n$$\\vec E=\\frac{\\vec F}{m},\\qquad E=-\\frac{dV}{dr}$$\n\n| Distribution | Field $E$ |\n|---|---|\n| Point mass / outside sphere | $GM/r^2$ |\n| Ring on axis | $\\dfrac{GMx}{(a^2+x^2)^{3/2}}$ (max at $x=a/\\sqrt2$) |\n| Inside spherical shell | 0 |\n| Inside uniform solid sphere | $\\dfrac{GMr}{a^3}$ ($\\propto r$) |\n\n## Variation of g\n\n6. At the surface $g=GM/R^2$. It varies with height, depth and latitude:\n\n$$g_h=\\frac{g}{(1+h/R)^2}\\approx g\\left(1-\\frac{2h}{R}\\right)\\ (h\\ll R)$$\n\n$$g_d=g\\left(1-\\frac dR\\right),\\qquad g'=g-\\omega^2R\\cos^2\\lambda$$\n\n($g\\propto r$ inside, $\\propto1/r^2$ outside.)\n\n7. $g$ is zero at the centre and maximum at the surface. $g$ at height $R$ is $g/4$, and at height $2R$ it is $g/9$.\n\n> $g$ changes with height or depth (very frequently asked): \"% change from depth $d$ to height $h$\", \"height where $g$ becomes $g/n$\", \"pendulum clock at altitude runs slow\".\n\n*Asked: 2023 • 31 Jan • Evening; 2024 • 29 Jan • Morning; 2026 • 5 Apr • Morning*\n\n## Planets, Satellites & Kepler's Laws\n\n8. Orbital speed and period of a satellite at distance $r$ from the centre (it does not depend on the satellite's mass). Gravity supplies $mv^2/r$; $r=R+h$.\n\n$$v_o=\\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{r}},\\qquad T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{r^3}{GM}}$$\n\n9. Kepler's laws: (1) orbits are ellipses with the sun at one focus; (2) a planet sweeps equal areas in equal times (conservation of angular momentum); (3) $T^2\\propto a^3$.\n\n$$\\frac{dA}{dt}=\\frac{L}{2m}=\\text{const},\\qquad T^2=\\frac{4\\pi^2}{GM}a^3$$\n\n10. Energy of a satellite: KE $=\\dfrac{GMm}{2r}$, PE $=-\\dfrac{GMm}{r}$, total $E=-\\dfrac{GMm}{2r}$ (bound). Binding energy $=+\\dfrac{GMm}{2r}$.\n\n$$E=-K=\\tfrac12U$$\n\n11. Geostationary satellite: period 24 h, equatorial orbit at height about 36000 km. Weightlessness in a satellite: everything inside is in free fall with the same acceleration, so normal forces are zero.\n\n> Kepler's 3rd law around different central masses: $T^2\\propto r^3/M$. A satellite skimming the surface has $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{R/g}=\\sqrt{3\\pi/G\\rho}$, which depends only on density (2026 statement question).\n\n*Asked: 2026 • 2 Apr • Morning; 2026 • 22 Jan • Evening; 2024 • 29 Jan • Evening*\n\n## Escape Velocity, Binding Energy, Black Holes\n\n12. Escape velocity: the minimum speed to reach infinity ($KE+PE\\ge0$). It is independent of the mass of the body and the direction of projection.\n\n$$v_e=\\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{R}}=\\sqrt{2gR}\\approx11.2\\ \\text{km/s},\\qquad v_e=\\sqrt2\\,v_o\\ \\text{(near surface)}$$\n\n13. A body falling from infinity reaches the earth with $v_e$ and KE $=mgR$. Energy needed to lift a body to height $h$: $\\Delta U=\\dfrac{mgh}{1+h/R}$ (to height $nR$: $\\dfrac{n}{n+1}mgR$).\n14. Gravitational binding energy of a uniform sphere: $\\dfrac{3GM^2}{5R}$. A black hole has $v_e>c$ (Schwarzschild radius $2GM/c^2$).\n15. Inertial mass ($F=ma$) and gravitational mass ($F=GMm/r^2$) are found experimentally to be equal (the basis of general relativity).\n\n> Escape/energy questions are the most asked group: energy to send a body to height $2R$ or to infinity, $v_e\\propto\\sqrt{M/R}$ for another planet, satellite energy when the orbit changes.\n\n*Asked: 2024 • 31 Jan • Evening; 2023 • 25 Jan • Evening; 2026 • 2 Apr • Evening*\n\n## JEE Main Focus\n\n**Question patterns asked again and again**\n- $g$ with height/depth, % change, pendulum at altitude — e.g. 2023 • 31 Jan • Evening; 2024 • 29 Jan • Morning; 2026 • 5 Apr • Morning\n- Escape velocity ratios for another planet — e.g. 2024 • 31 Jan • Evening; 2023 • 12 Apr • Morning\n- Period change with radius ($T^2\\propto r^3$) — e.g. 2024 • 29 Jan • Evening; 2023 • 29 Jan • Evening\n- Satellite energy (KE, PE, total; orbit transfer) — e.g. 2023 • 8 Apr • Morning; 2026 • 21 Jan • Morning\n- PE increase to height $2R$ ($\\tfrac23mgR$) — e.g. 2023 • 25 Jan • Evening; 2026 • 5 Apr • Evening\n- Field/force of masses at polygon corners (superposition) — e.g. 2024 • 31 Jan • Morning; 2026 • 22 Jan • Morning\n\n**Concepts combined in one question**\n- Gravitation + SHM (tunnel through the earth: $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{R/g}$, independent of the chord)\n- Kepler + angular momentum ($L=m\\sqrt{GMr}$)\n\n**Tricky points**\n- A pendulum clock at altitude runs slow ($g$ decreases).\n- Escape velocity doesn't depend on the angle of projection.\n\n**Mistakes to avoid**\n- Using $mgh$ for $h\\sim R$.\n- Using the approximation $1-2h/R$ when $h$ is not small.\n\n**Asked in April 2026**\n- 2 Apr S1 • Q30 — Kepler's 3rd law with different star masses: T² ∝ R³/M\n- 2 Apr S2 • Q31 — Body falling from infinity reaches v = √(2gR) = 11.2 km/s; KE = mgR\n- 4 Apr S2 • Q30 — Height where g becomes g/9: h = 2R\n- 5 Apr S1 • Q28 — % change in g from depth d to height h: (d + 2h)/R\n- 5 Apr S2 • Q32 — ΔU from surface to height 2R = (2/3)mgR\n"
