    "rawMarkdown": "# Mechanical Properties of Solids\n\n- Class: 11 Physics\n- Chapter no.: 9\n- Weightage: High\n\n## Elasticity, Stress & Strain\n\n1. Interatomic forces act like springs. Elasticity is the property of regaining shape when the deforming force is removed. Perfectly plastic bodies don't regain it.\n2. Stress = internal restoring force per unit area. Types: longitudinal (tensile/compressive), shearing, bulk (volume).\n\n$$\\text{stress}=\\frac FA\\ \\ (\\text{N m}^{-2})$$\n\n3. Strain (dimensionless): longitudinal $\\Delta l/l$, shearing $\\theta\\approx x/l$, volume $\\Delta V/V$.\n\n## Hooke's Law & Elastic Moduli\n\n4. Hooke's law: within the elastic limit, stress ∝ strain. The constant is the modulus of elasticity.\n\n$$Y=\\frac{F/A}{\\Delta l/l}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\Delta l=\\frac{Fl}{AY}$$\n\n$$B=-\\frac{\\Delta P}{\\Delta V/V},\\qquad \\eta=\\frac{F/A}{\\theta}$$\n\n(For a wire of length $l$ loaded by $Mg$: $Y=\\dfrac{Mg/A}{\\Delta l/l}$.)\n\n5. Compressibility $=1/B$. For gases: isothermal $B=P$, adiabatic $B=\\gamma P$. Poisson's ratio $\\sigma=-\\dfrac{\\Delta d/d}{\\Delta l/l}$ (theory: $-1$ to $0.5$).\n\n$$Y=2\\eta(1+\\sigma)=3B(1-2\\sigma)$$\n\n6. Stress–strain curve: proportional limit → elastic limit → yield point → plastic region → ultimate strength → fracture. Ductile materials have a long plastic region, brittle ones break soon after the elastic limit. Elastomers (rubber) have no linear region. The slope of the linear part = $Y$.\n7. Breaking stress depends only on the material. Max load = breaking stress × area.\n8. Wire hanging under its own weight: $\\Delta l=\\dfrac{Mgl}{2AY}$ (half of a load $Mg$ at the end). Thermal stress in a clamped rod heated by $\\Delta T$: $Y\\alpha\\Delta T$.\n\n> $Y$, $B$ and $\\eta$ are material constants. Doubling the length or radius changes $\\Delta l$, not $Y$. $Y$ decreases with temperature.\n\n> Elongation questions (series wires, two wires with blocks, own weight, ratio when $L$ and $d$ change) are the most asked elasticity questions. Breaking stress with a lift accelerating, and bulk modulus with depth ($\\Delta V/V=\\rho gh/B$) are also asked.\n\n*Asked: 2025 • 7 Apr • Morning; 2026 • 4 Apr • Evening; 2026 • 6 Apr • Morning; 2025 • 29 Jan • Morning*\n\n## Elastic Potential Energy & Measuring Y\n\n9. Energy stored in a stretched wire (area under the $F$–$\\Delta l$ graph):\n\n$$U=\\tfrac12F\\Delta l=\\tfrac12\\,\\text{stress}\\times\\text{strain}\\times\\text{volume}$$\n\n$$u=\\frac UV=\\tfrac12Y(\\text{strain})^2=\\frac{(\\text{stress})^2}{2Y}$$\n\n10. Searle's method (Experimental Skill): $Y=\\dfrac{Mgl}{\\pi r^2\\Delta l}$. Plot load vs extension, take the slope, and use a reference wire to cancel temperature and sag effects.\n\n## JEE Main Focus\n\n**Question patterns asked again and again**\n- Elongation ratios / series wires / own weight — e.g. 2025 • 7 Apr • Morning; 2023 • 30 Jan • Evening; 2021 • 31 Aug • Morning\n- Bulk modulus: % volume change, compression at depth — e.g. 2026 • 4 Apr • Morning; 2025 • 29 Jan • Morning\n- Breaking stress / safe load — e.g. 2026 • 6 Apr • Morning; 2021 • 26 Aug • Evening\n- Surface energy of drops/bubbles — e.g. 2022 • 28 Jun • Morning; 2025 • 2 Apr • Evening\n- Terminal velocity scaling — e.g. 2024 • 31 Jan • Evening; 2023 • 11 Apr • Evening\n- Capillary rise changes — e.g. 2025 • 7 Apr • Evening; 2023 • 30 Jan • Morning\n\n**Concepts combined in one question**\n- Elasticity + thermal expansion (thermal stress)\n- Elasticity + error analysis (error in $Y$)\n- Surface tension + heat (temperature rise on merging)\n- Stokes + buoyancy\n\n**Tricky points**\n- A soap bubble has two surfaces: $4T/R$ and $2T\\Delta A$.\n- Stress at a point in a heavy wire = (load + weight of the part below)/A.\n- Isothermal bulk modulus of a gas = $P$.\n\n**Mistakes to avoid**\n- Forgetting 1 poise = 0.1 Pa s.\n- Using diameter instead of radius in Stokes' law.\n\n**Asked in April 2026**\n- 2 Apr S1 • Q45 — Drop splits into 512 droplets: ΔU = T•ΔA (surface energy)\n- 2 Apr S1 • Q49 — Stress at a point in heavy wire with load: (W + w•x/l)/A\n- 2 Apr S2 • Q33 — Work to enlarge soap bubble: W = T•2•4π(r₂²−r₁²)\n- 2 Apr S2 • Q35 — Stokes' law, terminal velocity of rising air bubble → η\n- 4 Apr S1 • Q27 — Bulk modulus: ΔV/V = ΔP/B\n- 4 Apr S1 • Q34 — Two wires in series: total elongation = Σ FL/(AY)\n- 4 Apr S1 • Q47 — Work to enlarge soap bubble (two surfaces)\n- 4 Apr S2 • Q31 — Two wires with blocks: different tensions, ΔL = TL/(AY)\n- 5 Apr S1 • Q31 — Young's modulus is a material property (independent of size)\n- 5 Apr S1 • Q43 — Breaking stress: max load before either wire breaks\n- 5 Apr S1 • Q46 — Shear modulus: displacement = F•L/(ηA)\n- 5 Apr S2 • Q33 — Eight drops coalesce: energy released\n- 5 Apr S2 • Q46 — Elastic PE = ½ × stress × strain × volume\n- 8 Apr S2 • Q34 — Terminal velocity ∝ r²: drop broken into 64\n"
