    "id": "10-mechanical-properties-of-fluids",
    "prefix": "10",
    "number": 10,
    "title": "Mechanical Properties of Fluids",
    "classTag": "11 Physics",
    "weightage": "High",
    "rawMarkdown": "# Mechanical Properties of Fluids\n\n- Class: 11 Physics\n- Chapter no.: 10\n- Weightage: High\n\n> Fluids at rest (pressure, Pascal, Archimedes) and fluids in motion (continuity, Bernoulli). Surface tension and viscosity are covered below.\n\n## Pressure in a Fluid & Pascal's Law\n\n1. A fluid at rest exerts force only perpendicular to any surface. Pressure = normal force per unit area (a scalar; 1 Pa = 1 N m⁻², 1 atm = $1.013\\times10^5$ Pa).\n\n$$P=\\frac{dF}{dA}$$\n\n2. Pressure increases with depth. Points at the same level in the same continuous liquid have the same pressure, whatever the shape of the vessel.\n\n$$P=P_0+\\rho gh$$\n\n3. Pascal's law: a pressure change applied to an enclosed liquid is transmitted undiminished to every point. Hydraulic lift / press:\n\n$$\\frac{F_1}{A_1}=\\frac{F_2}{A_2}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ F_2=F_1\\frac{A_2}{A_1}$$\n\n4. In a container accelerating up with $a$: $P=P_0+\\rho(g+a)h$. Accelerating horizontally: the free surface tilts, $\\tan\\theta=a/g$.\n\n## Atmospheric Pressure & Barometer\n\n5. A mercury barometer holds a column of height $h$ where $P_0=\\rho_{Hg}gh$ (76 cm at 1 atm). Manometer: pressure difference $=\\rho gh$ (gauge pressure). Absolute pressure = atmospheric + gauge.\n\n## Archimedes' Principle & Buoyancy\n\n6. A body (wholly or partly) immersed in a fluid feels an upward buoyant force equal to the weight of the fluid displaced, acting at the centre of buoyancy.\n\n$$F_B=\\rho_{fluid}V_{sub}\\,g$$\n\n7. Floating: weight = buoyant force ⇒ fraction submerged $=\\rho_{body}/\\rho_{liquid}$. Apparent weight in a liquid $=mg-F_B$. Relative density = weight in air / loss of weight in water.\n\n$$\\frac{V_{sub}}{V}=\\frac{\\rho_{body}}{\\rho_{liquid}}$$\n\n8. Extra load $m$ on a floating body sinks it further by a volume $m/\\rho_{liquid}$.\n\n> Floating cube + coin: the extra submerged depth × area × $\\rho$ gives the coin's mass (2026). Hydraulic lift and flotation are also asked.\n\n*Asked: 2026 • 2 Apr • Morning; 2023 • 8 Apr • Evening; 2025 • 28 Jan • Evening*\n\n## Fluid Flow & Equation of Continuity\n\n9. Steady (streamline) flow: the velocity at a point doesn't change with time, and streamlines never cross. Turbulent flow is irregular (above the critical velocity). Ideal fluid: incompressible, non-viscous, irrotational. Streamlines crowd where the flow is faster.\n10. Continuity (mass conservation, incompressible fluid):\n\n$$A_1v_1=A_2v_2=\\text{volume flow rate}$$\n\nNarrow section: faster flow ($v_2>v_1$).\n\n> A spray gun with $n$ holes: $Av=nav'$. Check cm² and mm² conversions.\n\n*Asked: 2026 • 4 Apr • Evening*\n\n## Bernoulli's Equation & Applications\n\n11. Along a streamline of an ideal fluid (energy conservation per unit volume):\n\n$$P+\\tfrac12\\rho v^2+\\rho gh=\\text{constant}$$\n\n12. Torricelli's theorem: speed of efflux from a hole at depth $h$ below the free surface:\n\n$$v=\\sqrt{2gh}$$\n\n$$\\text{range on ground}=2\\sqrt{h(H-h)}\\quad(\\text{max }H\\text{ at }h=H/2)$$\n\n13. Venturimeter: the pressure difference across the throat gives the flow rate $v_1=A_2\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2\\Delta P}{\\rho(A_1^2-A_2^2)}}$. Other uses: the Pitot tube, the lift on an aeroplane wing, an atomiser, a roof blown off in a storm, the Magnus effect. (Throat: higher speed, lower pressure.)\n\n> Bernoulli + continuity (same level: $\\Delta P=\\tfrac12\\rho(v_2^2-v_1^2)$) and Torricelli + projectile range appear 3–5 times a year.\n\n*Asked: 2026 • 8 Apr • Evening; 2026 • 6 Apr • Evening; 2023 • 13 Apr • Morning; 2022 • 28 Jul • Evening*\n\n## Surface Tension & Excess Pressure\n\n14. Surface tension $T$: force per unit length acting along the surface, perpendicular to an imaginary line on it. It equals the surface energy per unit area.\n\n$$T=\\frac Fl=\\frac{\\Delta W}{\\Delta A}\\quad(\\text{N m}^{-1}=\\text{J m}^{-2})$$\n\nA film has two surfaces: $F=2Tl$.\n\n15. Soap film / bubble has two surfaces: the work to increase the film area by $\\Delta A$ is $2T\\Delta A$.\n\n$$W_{bubble}=2T\\cdot4\\pi\\left(r_2^2-r_1^2\\right)=8\\pi T\\left(r_2^2-r_1^2\\right)$$\n\n16. Excess pressure inside a curved surface:\n\n$$\\text{drop: }\\Delta P=\\frac{2T}{R},\\qquad \\text{bubble (2 surfaces): }\\Delta P=\\frac{4T}{R}$$\n\n17. Splitting a drop of radius $R$ into $n$ equal droplets: $r=R/n^{1/3}$. The energy needed equals the increase in surface area × $T$. Merging releases the same energy (and heats the drop).\n\n$$\\Delta E=4\\pi R^2T\\left(n^{1/3}-1\\right)$$\n\n> Surface energy (splitting/merging drops, blowing bubbles) is a top fluid topic: 5 of the 8 April-2026 shifts you uploaded had one. Remember the factor 2 for bubbles.\n\n*Asked: 2026 • 2 Apr • Morning; 2026 • 5 Apr • Evening; 2022 • 28 Jun • Morning; 2025 • 2 Apr • Evening; 2023 • 1 Feb • Morning*\n\n## Contact Angle & Capillary Rise\n\n18. Contact angle $\\theta$ (measured inside the liquid): acute → the liquid wets the solid and rises (water–glass ≈ 0°). Obtuse → it doesn't wet and falls (mercury–glass ≈ 135°). It depends on the solid–liquid pair, not on the tube radius.\n19. Capillary rise (Jurin's law):\n\n$$h=\\frac{2T\\cos\\theta}{r\\rho g}\\qquad(hr=\\text{constant})$$\n\n20. In a tube shorter than $h$ the liquid does not overflow: the meniscus radius adjusts so that $R'h'=Rh$. Detergents lower $T$, so the rise is smaller (Experimental Skill).\n\n> Capillary questions: change in rise with radius or $T$, mercury depression, A-R on why oil spreads (contact angle).\n\n*Asked: 2025 • 7 Apr • Evening; 2023 • 30 Jan • Morning; 2022 • 29 Jul • Morning*\n\n## Viscosity, Stokes' Law, Terminal Velocity\n\n21. Viscosity: internal friction between fluid layers moving relative to each other. Newton's law:\n\n$$F=-\\eta A\\frac{dv}{dx}\\quad(\\eta\\text{ in Pa s};\\ 1\\text{ poise}=0.1\\text{ Pa s})$$\n\n22. Poiseuille's equation (flow through a narrow tube of radius $r$, length $l$). The velocity profile is parabolic, fastest on the axis.\n\n$$Q=\\frac{\\pi Pr^4}{8\\eta l}$$\n\n23. Stokes' law: viscous drag on a sphere moving slowly: $F=6\\pi\\eta rv$. At terminal speed: $mg=B+6\\pi\\eta rv_t$.\n24. Terminal velocity: weight = buoyancy + drag.\n\n$$v_t=\\frac{2r^2(\\rho-\\sigma)g}{9\\eta}$$\n\n25. $v_t\\propto r^2$. An air bubble rising through a liquid uses $(\\rho_{liquid}-0)$. Drops merging into one big drop: $v_t$ increases by $n^{2/3}$.\n26. Critical velocity & Reynolds number: flow is streamline when $Re=\\dfrac{\\rho vD}{\\eta}$ is small (< ~2000) and turbulent when large (> ~3000).\n\n> Viscosity / terminal velocity is the most asked fluid topic: drop broken into 64 droplets ($v_t$ falls by 16), bubble rising, viscosity from steady fall.\n\n*Asked: 2026 • 8 Apr • Evening; 2026 • 2 Apr • Evening; 2024 • 31 Jan • Evening; 2025 • 3 Apr • Evening*\n\n## JEE Main Focus\n\n**Question patterns asked again and again**\n- Bernoulli in a horizontal tube with continuity — e.g. 2023 • 13 Apr • Morning; 2026 • 8 Apr • Evening\n- Torricelli efflux and range — e.g. 2026 • 6 Apr • Evening; 2022 • 28 Jul • Evening\n- Pascal / hydraulic lift / flotation, relative density — e.g. 2023 • 8 Apr • Evening; 2025 • 28 Jan • Evening\n- Assertion–reason: toothpaste tube (Pascal) — e.g. 2023 • 6 Apr • Evening\n\n**Tricky points**\n- Efflux with extra pressure on top: $v=\\sqrt{2(\\Delta P/\\rho+gh)}$.\n- The buoyant force depends on the displaced volume, not on the body's density.\n\n**Mistakes to avoid**\n- Using gauge pressure where absolute pressure is needed (and the other way round).\n\n**Asked in April 2026**\n- 2 Apr S1 • Q50 — Floating cube + coin: extra buoyancy = coin weight\n- 4 Apr S2 • Q32 — Continuity: A₁v₁ = n•a•v₂\n- 6 Apr S2 • Q34 — Torricelli efflux + projectile: range = 2√(h(H−h))\n- 8 Apr S2 • Q33 — Bernoulli + continuity: ΔP = ½ρ(v₂² − v₁²)\n"
